1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,763
That's a steel spacecraft
falling back from space.

2
00:00:03,783 --> 00:00:06,763
And the thing waiting for
it is a tower with arms.

3
00:00:07,113 --> 00:00:08,961
This hasn't happened yet.

4
00:00:08,981 --> 00:00:13,116
It's an illustration of what
SpaceX says it will try next month.

5
00:00:13,616 --> 00:00:17,583
SpaceX has already caught a falling
rocket booster with this tower.

6
00:00:17,603 --> 00:00:18,811
More than once.

7
00:00:19,111 --> 00:00:21,374
Now it wants to catch the other half,

8
00:00:21,394 --> 00:00:23,888
the spacecraft coming back from orbit.

9
00:00:24,188 --> 00:00:27,526
And that second catch is a
completely different problem.

10
00:00:28,326 --> 00:00:29,947
Here's the whole machine.

11
00:00:29,967 --> 00:00:35,139
It's called Starship, and stacked up
it stands about four hundred feet tall.

12
00:00:35,439 --> 00:00:38,420
The bottom part is the
booster, Super Heavy.

13
00:00:38,440 --> 00:00:42,829
Thirty three engines, and one job:
throw everything upward for the first

14
00:00:42,849 --> 00:00:44,137
few minutes.

15
00:00:44,437 --> 00:00:46,260
The top part is the ship.

16
00:00:46,280 --> 00:00:49,046
It has its own engines, carries the cargo,

17
00:00:49,066 --> 00:00:51,058
and actually goes to space.

18
00:00:51,358 --> 00:00:54,212
Rockets like this usually
end up in the ocean.

19
00:00:54,232 --> 00:00:59,202
So far, every Starship ship that has
flown has ended its flight there.

20
00:00:59,502 --> 00:01:03,912
On the next flight, Elon Musk says
they'll try to catch both pieces.

21
00:01:03,932 --> 00:01:08,645
He says it would be the first time an
orbital rocket has been fully recovered.

22
00:01:09,445 --> 00:01:11,422
So how do you catch a booster?

23
00:01:11,442 --> 00:01:15,641
First, the stages separate, a
couple of minutes after launch.

24
00:01:15,941 --> 00:01:19,586
The booster flips around and
relights some of its engines.

25
00:01:19,606 --> 00:01:21,377
That's the boostback burn.

26
00:01:21,397 --> 00:01:24,820
It sends the booster back
toward the launch site.

27
00:01:25,120 --> 00:01:28,493
Then it falls, engines
off, steering with these:

28
00:01:28,513 --> 00:01:32,871
grid fins. Steel lattices that
bite into the air like rudders.

29
00:01:33,171 --> 00:01:36,896
Close to the ground, the engines
light again for the landing burn,

30
00:01:36,916 --> 00:01:40,128
and the booster slides
sideways toward the tower.

31
00:01:40,428 --> 00:01:43,382
And here's the part most
people picture wrong.

32
00:01:43,402 --> 00:01:46,399
The tower doesn't snatch
a rocket out of the sky.

33
00:01:46,699 --> 00:01:49,731
The booster flies itself
between two open arms,

34
00:01:49,751 --> 00:01:52,650
called chopsticks. The arms close in,

35
00:01:52,670 --> 00:01:57,405
and it settles until load points
near its top come to rest on them.

36
00:01:57,705 --> 00:02:02,067
From that moment, the whole weight of
the booster hangs on a few small contact

37
00:02:02,087 --> 00:02:03,001
points.

38
00:02:03,301 --> 00:02:07,239
SpaceX first pulled this off
in October twenty twenty four,

39
00:02:07,259 --> 00:02:09,154
and has done it again since.

40
00:02:09,454 --> 00:02:13,385
On the new version three booster,
that interface got redesigned.

41
00:02:13,405 --> 00:02:15,922
Four grid fins became three bigger ones,

42
00:02:15,942 --> 00:02:19,581
and the catch points now
sit on the fins themselves.

43
00:02:19,881 --> 00:02:22,315
The new tower got shorter arms, too,

44
00:02:22,335 --> 00:02:25,683
so they can move faster to
track a vehicle coming in.

45
00:02:26,483 --> 00:02:28,671
So why is the ship so much harder?

46
00:02:28,971 --> 00:02:31,321
Because the booster never really leaves.

47
00:02:31,341 --> 00:02:35,616
It turns back after a few minutes,
and it's home in about seven.

48
00:02:35,916 --> 00:02:37,602
The ship keeps going.

49
00:02:37,622 --> 00:02:40,756
And getting to orbit isn't
mostly about going up.

50
00:02:40,776 --> 00:02:42,745
It's about going sideways.

51
00:02:43,045 --> 00:02:45,421
Fast enough that as you fall toward Earth,

52
00:02:45,441 --> 00:02:47,506
the ground curves away beneath you.

53
00:02:47,526 --> 00:02:50,300
Roughly seventeen thousand miles per hour.

54
00:02:50,600 --> 00:02:54,332
On the last flight, the ship
reached orbit for the first time,

55
00:02:54,352 --> 00:02:58,126
and came down in the Pacific
about three hours after launch.

56
00:02:58,426 --> 00:03:01,505
To land, all of that
speed has to go somewhere.

57
00:03:01,525 --> 00:03:03,624
Mostly, it goes into the air.

58
00:03:04,424 --> 00:03:06,473
The ship comes in belly first.

59
00:03:06,493 --> 00:03:09,764
That whole side is covered
in black heat shield tiles,

60
00:03:09,784 --> 00:03:12,314
and it faces straight into the flow.

61
00:03:12,614 --> 00:03:16,585
The air in front of it is crushed
so hard it becomes glowing plasma,

62
00:03:16,605 --> 00:03:20,293
while the steel on the other side
hides in the shield's shadow.

63
00:03:20,593 --> 00:03:22,470
No engines steer it here.

64
00:03:22,490 --> 00:03:26,662
The ship uses four flaps, two
near the nose and two at the tail,

65
00:03:26,682 --> 00:03:29,765
like a skydiver using arms and legs.

66
00:03:30,065 --> 00:03:33,584
It stays in that belly flop all the
way down through the atmosphere,

67
00:03:33,604 --> 00:03:35,935
until most of its speed is gone.

68
00:03:36,235 --> 00:03:38,886
Then comes the strangest move in rocketry.

69
00:03:39,186 --> 00:03:43,832
The engines light, the ship swings
from horizontal to vertical in seconds,

70
00:03:43,852 --> 00:03:45,717
and burns to slow down.

71
00:03:46,017 --> 00:03:48,812
SpaceX has done this flip over the ocean.

72
00:03:48,832 --> 00:03:52,406
What it has never done is end
it inside the arms of a tower.

73
00:03:53,206 --> 00:03:57,644
Which brings us to the tower, because
the tower has to meet the ship halfway.

74
00:03:57,944 --> 00:04:00,118
This is the launch tower at Starbase,

75
00:04:00,138 --> 00:04:02,852
in Texas. It isn't scaffolding.

76
00:04:02,872 --> 00:04:04,065
It's a machine.

77
00:04:04,365 --> 00:04:08,370
The chopsticks hang from a carriage
that rides up and down the tower,

78
00:04:08,390 --> 00:04:10,901
and the arms swing open and closed,

79
00:04:10,921 --> 00:04:15,094
fast enough to follow a vehicle
that isn't perfectly lined up.

80
00:04:15,394 --> 00:04:20,001
For the ship, the arms aim for catch
points up near the forward flaps.

81
00:04:20,021 --> 00:04:23,087
The exact hardware hasn't
been shown in detail,

82
00:04:23,107 --> 00:04:26,481
so treat this as a
diagram, not a blueprint.

83
00:04:26,781 --> 00:04:29,408
In September, SpaceX rehearsed.

84
00:04:29,428 --> 00:04:33,353
It lifted the actual ship for this
flight with the new tower's arms,

85
00:04:33,373 --> 00:04:35,687
into what looked like a catch position,

86
00:04:35,707 --> 00:04:37,486
and held it there.

87
00:04:37,786 --> 00:04:40,151
That tests loads and alignment.

88
00:04:40,171 --> 00:04:44,940
It doesn't test the hard part: doing
it with a ship that's still flying.

89
00:04:45,240 --> 00:04:49,266
For the catch to work, everything
has to agree at the same instant.

90
00:04:49,286 --> 00:04:51,591
The ship's height. Its drift.

91
00:04:51,611 --> 00:04:53,666
Its tilt. Its speed.

92
00:04:53,686 --> 00:04:57,400
And the arms in the right
place, closing around it.

93
00:04:57,700 --> 00:05:01,789
Two enormous machines, one flying
and one bolted to the ground,

94
00:05:01,809 --> 00:05:04,678
meeting at a few small points of contact.

95
00:05:05,478 --> 00:05:06,840
So what could go wrong?

96
00:05:07,140 --> 00:05:09,606
The ship could come in
a little off target.

97
00:05:09,626 --> 00:05:11,917
It's flying through wind on engine power,

98
00:05:11,937 --> 00:05:15,005
with only so much control left at the end.

99
00:05:15,305 --> 00:05:18,219
A few feet of drift
doesn't sound like much.

100
00:05:18,239 --> 00:05:20,642
But if the catch points miss the arms,

101
00:05:20,662 --> 00:05:24,366
nothing else on the ship is
built to hold its weight there.

102
00:05:24,666 --> 00:05:26,640
Or the tower might not be ready.

103
00:05:26,660 --> 00:05:28,419
That's actually happened.

104
00:05:28,439 --> 00:05:32,161
On one earlier flight, the catch was
called off with the booster on its

105
00:05:32,181 --> 00:05:37,247
way home, because automated checks
on the tower didn't come back clean.

106
00:05:37,547 --> 00:05:39,952
The booster went into the Gulf instead.

107
00:05:39,972 --> 00:05:41,746
That's the system working.

108
00:05:41,766 --> 00:05:45,796
If anything looks wrong, the
vehicle doesn't try for the tower.

109
00:05:46,096 --> 00:05:49,419
And the ship arrives having
just survived reentry.

110
00:05:49,439 --> 00:05:53,408
Whatever state it's in, it has
to fly that landing anyway.

111
00:05:53,708 --> 00:05:57,611
Musk says this first attempt is
more likely than not to work.

112
00:05:57,631 --> 00:06:01,702
But the ship for this flight was
recently sent back for more testing,

113
00:06:01,722 --> 00:06:03,991
so the date could still move.

114
00:06:04,791 --> 00:06:07,188
If it does work, here's the sequence.

115
00:06:07,488 --> 00:06:09,456
Launch. Separation.

116
00:06:09,476 --> 00:06:13,639
The booster turns around, and
minutes later it's back in the arms.

117
00:06:13,939 --> 00:06:18,626
Then, after its time in orbit, the
ship comes home on its own schedule,

118
00:06:18,646 --> 00:06:21,635
to a tower with its arms open and waiting.

119
00:06:21,935 --> 00:06:24,161
Two catches, from a single launch.

120
00:06:24,461 --> 00:06:26,194
And that's the real point.

121
00:06:26,214 --> 00:06:28,313
Catching it once is a stunt.

122
00:06:28,333 --> 00:06:31,632
SpaceX wants to set both
pieces back on the pad,

123
00:06:31,652 --> 00:06:35,567
refuel them, and fly them
again, like an airliner.

124
00:06:35,867 --> 00:06:38,023
It hasn't reflown a ship yet.

125
00:06:38,043 --> 00:06:41,897
Not once. Even a perfect
catch is only step one.

126
00:06:42,197 --> 00:06:46,588
But for decades, the most expensive
part of every rocket was the part you

127
00:06:46,608 --> 00:06:47,604
threw away.

128
00:06:47,904 --> 00:06:51,621
SpaceX is trying to make sure
there's nothing left to throw.
